To begin the implementation, include the necessary headers such as iostream and unordered_map. Inside the main function, initialize the array provided in the prompt to prepare for the iteration process that will populate our frequency map.

The logic proceeds by iterating through every element in the array and incrementing its value within the hash map. Once the map is fully populated, we iterate through it once more to identify which key possesses the maximum frequency value found during the initial count.

We must define a temporary variable to track the maximum frequency encountered and another to store the actual element associated with that frequency. This ensures that even if multiple elements share high frequencies, we can reliably capture the most frequent one found in the set.

#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>
int main() {
    int arr[] = {1, 5, 3, 6, 2, 3, 5, 8, 10, 5, 1, 2, 3, 6, 1, 10};
    std::unordered_map<int, int> counts;
    for(int n : arr) counts[n]++;
    int maxEl = -1, maxFreq = 0;
    for(auto const& [val, freq] : counts) {
        if(freq > maxFreq) { maxFreq = freq; maxEl = val; }
    }
    std::cout << "Unique: " << counts.size() << " Max element: " << maxEl << std::endl;
    return 0;
}

